### What is the grpc-spring-boot-starter?

The `grpc-spring-boot-starter` (commonly the `net.devh` project) auto-configures an embedded gRPC server on a configured port, separate from the HTTP port, and scans for beans annotated with `@GrpcService`. It also manages gRPC client channels and interceptors.

```xml
<!-- pom.xml dependencies -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>net.devh</groupId>
    <artifactId>grpc-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
    <version>3.1.0.RELEASE</version>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.grpc</groupId>
    <artifactId>grpc-protobuf</artifactId>
    <version>1.63.0</version>
</dependency>
```

### How do you define a .proto file for banking services?

You define messages and RPC methods in a `.proto` file. The protobuf Maven or Gradle plugin generates Java stubs and server base classes from it.

```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";
package com.bank.grpc;

option java_package = "com.bank.grpc";
option java_outer_classname = "TransferProto";

service TransferService {
  rpc Transfer (TransferRequest) returns (TransferResponse);
}

message TransferRequest {
  string from_account = 1;
  string to_account = 2;
  string amount = 3; // String for BigDecimal safety
}

message TransferResponse {
  bool success = 1;
  string message = 2;
  string new_balance = 3;
}
```

### How do you implement the server with @GrpcService?

Your server class extends the generated `ImplBase` and carries `@GrpcService`. The starter registers it automatically with no bootstrapping code.

```java
package com.bank.grpc;

import io.grpc.stub.StreamObserver;
import net.devh.boot.grpc.server.service.GrpcService;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@GrpcService
public class TransferGrpcService
        extends TransferServiceGrpc.TransferServiceImplBase {

    @Override
    public void transfer(
            TransferRequest request,
            StreamObserver<TransferResponse> responseObserver) {

        BigDecimal amount = new BigDecimal(request.getAmount());
        String from = request.getFromAccount();
        String to = request.getToAccount();

        // Business logic: debit from, credit to
        boolean success = processTransfer(from, to, amount);

        TransferResponse response = TransferResponse.newBuilder()
                .setSuccess(success)
                .setMessage(success ? "Transfer complete" : "Insufficient funds")
                .setNewBalance("250000.00")
                .build();

        responseObserver.onNext(response);
        responseObserver.onCompleted();
    }

    private boolean processTransfer(
            String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Rajesh transfers to Priya
        System.out.println("Transfer " + amount + " from " + from
                + " to " + to);
        return true;
    }
}
```

### How do you inject and use a client stub with @GrpcClient?

You inject a stub into any Spring bean with `@GrpcClient("service-name")`. A matching entry in `application.yml` provides the address and negotiation type.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.grpc.TransferProto.TransferRequest;
import com.bank.grpc.TransferProto.TransferResponse;
import com.bank.grpc.TransferServiceGrpc;
import net.devh.boot.grpc.client.inject.GrpcClient;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    @GrpcClient("transfer-service")
    private TransferServiceGrpc.TransferServiceBlockingStub stub;

    public String initiateTransfer(
            String from, String to, String amount) {
        TransferRequest request = TransferRequest.newBuilder()
                .setFromAccount(from)
                .setToAccount(to)
                .setAmount(amount)
                .build();

        TransferResponse response = stub.transfer(request);
        return response.getMessage();
    }
}
```

```yaml
# application.yml
grpc:
  client:
    transfer-service:
      address: 'dns:///transfer-service:9090'
      negotiationType: plaintext
```

### How does service discovery integrate?

Instead of a static address, you point the client at a discovery-backed address. With Eureka or Consul the starter resolves `discovery:///transfer-service` to live registered instances. With Kubernetes, a headless service DNS returns all pod IPs so the client can client-side load balance across them.

```yaml
# Kubernetes headless service for gRPC discovery
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: transfer-service
spec:
  clusterIP: None  # headless: returns all pod IPs
  selector:
    app: transfer-service
  ports:
    - port: 9090
      targetPort: 9090
      protocol: TCP
```

### How does gRPC health checking work?

The gRPC Health Checking Protocol defines a `Health` service with a `Check` method returning SERVING or NOT_SERVING and a `Watch` for streaming updates. The starter can expose this automatically.

```java
package com.bank.grpc;

import io.grpc.health.v1.HealthCheckResponse;
import io.grpc.health.v1.HealthGrpc;
import io.grpc.health.v1.HealthCheckRequest;
import io.grpc.stub.StreamObserver;
import net.devh.boot.grpc.server.service.GrpcService;

@GrpcService
public class BankHealthService
        extends HealthGrpc.HealthImplBase {

    private volatile boolean databaseReady = false;

    @Override
    public void check(
            HealthCheckRequest request,
            StreamObserver<HealthCheckResponse> observer) {

        HealthCheckResponse.ServingStatus status = databaseReady
                ? HealthCheckResponse.ServingStatus.SERVING
                : HealthCheckResponse.ServingStatus.NOT_SERVING;

        observer.onNext(HealthCheckResponse.newBuilder()
                .setStatus(status).build());
        observer.onCompleted();
    }

    public void setDatabaseReady(boolean ready) {
        this.databaseReady = ready;
    }
}
```

### How do Kubernetes gRPC probes work?

From Kubernetes 1.24 onward, a native gRPC probe calls the Health service directly instead of needing a custom exec script.

```yaml
# Pod spec with gRPC probes
containers:
  - name: transfer-service
    ports:
      - containerPort: 9090
    readinessProbe:
      grpc:
        port: 9090
      initialDelaySeconds: 5
      periodSeconds: 10
    livenessProbe:
      grpc:
        port: 9090
      initialDelaySeconds: 10
      periodSeconds: 30
```

### Why prefer client-side load balancing for gRPC?

gRPC uses long-lived HTTP/2 connections. A single connection through an L4 load balancer pins all calls to one pod. Client-side load balancing with a Kubernetes headless service returns all pod IPs, and the client opens a subchannel to each, rotating requests across them.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import io.grpc.ManagedChannel;
import io.grpc.ManagedChannelBuilder;

public class GrpcChannelConfig {

    public ManagedChannel createBalancedChannel() {
        return ManagedChannelBuilder
                .forTarget("dns:///transfer-service:9090")
                .defaultLoadBalancingPolicy("round_robin")
                .usePlaintext()
                .build();
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Tie the health status to real readiness: report NOT_SERVING while the database connection pool is still warming up, so traffic is not routed to a pod that will fail. Prefer client-side load balancing with the Kubernetes headless service for gRPC, because a single long-lived HTTP/2 connection through an L4 load balancer would otherwise pin all calls to one pod. Use global and per-client interceptors for cross-cutting concerns like authentication and logging.
